      "id": "cmtfewhlf000104jrnvpa8qoi",
      "term": "Can you describe how deferred revenue might be adjusted in a merger model?",
      "setTitle": "M&A ",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "Deferred revenue is a liability representing cash already collected for goods or services not yet delivered.",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Historically, the buyer wrote acquired deferred revenue down to fair value — approximately the cost to perform plus a small profit margin.",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Under the old fair-value treatment, less revenue was recognized after close because the liability covered only cost plus margin, not the full contract value.",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "The old write-down reduced the acquired deferred revenue balance on the opening balance sheet, and that reduction increased the amount allocated to goodwill.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "The old fair-value approach changed under ASU 2021-08 because it frequently contradicted ASC 606.",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "The old fair-value approach made combined-company revenue hard to predict.",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "Under ASU 2021-08, contract liabilities are no longer written down — they carry over at the target's book value.",
          "kind": "contrast"
        },
        {
          "text": "Revenue is recognized under the same ASC 606 principles the target already applied, flowing exactly as it would have on a standalone basis.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Under ASU 2021-08, no purchase accounting adjustment touches goodwill.",
          "kind": "contrast"
        }
      ],
